+# Decision: How committed changes reach browsers
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS and the server assigns each committed edit one event revision. This decision covers the other direction: how a committed revision gets from the server to the browsers displaying that event.
+
+The choice shapes three design nodes at once. The change store, delivery, and browser sync each need different mechanisms depending on the transport, so they are sized around this decision and would all be reworked if it were reversed.
+
+Several things hold whichever transport is used, and so do not help choose between them:
+
+- Every option can meet the goal of showing committed changes within 15 seconds under normal connectivity.
+- Every option must recover the latest board after a browser has been disconnected for up to 20 minutes, and must make a connectivity failure visible rather than letting it look like an unchanged board.
+- Event updates contain stall IDs and operational statuses, not customer records, and the tenets permit both self-hosted and managed infrastructure. Nothing rules out sending updates through a third party.
+
+The scale is 800 simultaneous browsers across 40 markets, with a busy market averaging two status changes per minute and opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No gate eliminates an option: all three satisfy the goals and tenets. The choice rests on weighted criteria.
+
+- **Update latency — major.** How soon a coordinator sees a closure, late arrival, or power problem after it is committed. The 15-second goal is the floor every option clears; this criterion is about how far below it an option gets. Stall status is acted on by people standing in a market, and a board that lags by seconds invites them to keep using the group chats it replaces.
+- **Vendor independence — major.** Whether the board's delivery path depends on one external provider: a recurring fee that scales with connections or messages, and delivery and browser code written against that provider's subscription model. The tenets leave self-hosted versus managed open, so this weight is a judgment made for this decision, not one inherited from a tenet.
+- **Operational burden — moderate.** What MarketBoard has to run and keep healthy during published event hours, measured against the 99.5 percent availability goal: connection capacity, long-lived connection state, and behavior under deploys and restarts.
+- **Build complexity across the three nodes — moderate.** How much mechanism the change store, delivery, and browser sync need, and how much of it is failure-path logic that is hard to test.
+- **Read load on the change store — minor.** Repeated reads that return nothing new. Minor because the pilot's scale keeps the absolute load small under every option.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Each browser asks the server every five seconds for the revisions after the last one it applied. The change store answers cursor queries; delivery stays plain request/response with no server-side connection state; the browser runs a polling loop. Recovery after a disconnection is the same request as normal operation, so there is no separate catch-up path, and a failed poll is an immediate, unambiguous signal of lost connectivity.
+
+- Update latency: **weak**. Up to five seconds of lag by construction, plus request time.
+- Vendor independence: **strong**. No external provider.
+- Operational burden: **strong**. Stateless servers behind ordinary HTTPS infrastructure; deploys and restarts affect no long-lived connections.
+- Build complexity: **strong**. One read path serves both live updates and recovery.
+- Read load: **weak**. 800 browsers polling every five seconds is 160 requests per second, nearly all returning no change at two edits per minute per market.
+
+Summary: the least to build and run, paid for with seconds of lag on every update.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+Each browser holds a WebSocket to MarketBoard's own servers for the event it is viewing, and the server pushes every committed revision to the sockets subscribed to that event. The change store keeps revisions replayable and publishes each committed revision to delivery reliably; delivery manages socket sessions, subscriptions, and fan-out; the browser reconnects after a drop, states the last revision it applied, and catches up before live pushes resume.
+
+- Update latency: **strong**. Revisions are pushed as they commit.
+- Vendor independence: **strong**. No external provider and no per-connection or per-message fee.
+- Operational burden: **weak**. MarketBoard owns capacity for 800 long-lived connections, their behavior across deploys and restarts, and the socket tier's share of the availability goal.
+- Build complexity: **weak**. Live push and catch-up are separate paths that must agree, and a revision committed but never published has to be impossible or detectable. Most of this logic sits on failure paths.
+- Read load: **strong**. Browsers read only on connect and reconnect.
+
+Summary: fast updates with no provider dependence, paid for by owning connection operations and reconnect correctness.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+The server publishes each committed revision to a third-party realtime service, which holds the browser connections and fans messages out. The change store reconciles what it committed against what was published, since publication is a call to an external system that can fail independently; delivery is a wrapper around the provider's API and credentials; the browser uses the provider's client library and subscription model, including its reconnect behavior.
+
+- Update latency: **strong**. Push latency comparable to self-hosted sockets.
+- Vendor independence: **weak**. A recurring fee, and delivery and browser code shaped by one provider's model; changing provider means rewriting both.
+- Operational burden: **strong**. Connection capacity and connection health belong to the provider. The provider's uptime becomes part of MarketBoard's availability.
+- Build complexity: **partial**. Connection handling is bought, not built, but publication reconciliation and catch-up beyond the provider's own message retention still have to be designed.
+- Read load: **strong**. Browsers read only on connect and reconnect.
+
+Summary: the quickest route to low latency with the least connection operations, paid for with fees and provider coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.**
+
+It is the only option that is strong on both major criteria. Polling gives up latency, the reason a coordinator would trust the board over a group chat. A hosted service delivers the same latency but gives up vendor independence. Self-hosted sockets are weak on the two moderate criteria, and that is the trade being accepted deliberately: MarketBoard takes on connection capacity and reconnect work to get fast updates without tying the board to a realtime vendor.
+
+The pilot's scale makes that cost bounded. The system needs 800 simultaneous connections in a single region, with multi-region operation a non-goal, so capacity is a matter of running the socket tier well, not of building a distributed connection fabric.
+
+No tenet decides this. The tenets explicitly permit both self-hosted and managed infrastructure; the weight on vendor independence is specific to this decision. Two tenets constrain how the choice is carried out: a committed edit keeps one authoritative event revision whether a browser receives it by live push or by catch-up, and a dropped socket must be shown as a connectivity failure.
+
+Committing to this obligates:
+
+- **Change store:** revisions replayable from any revision a browser may still hold, and publication to delivery that cannot silently lose a committed revision.
+- **Delivery:** socket session management, per-event subscription and fan-out, and connection capacity for 800 browsers, including behavior across deploys and restarts.
+- **Browser sync:** reconnection, catch-up from the last applied revision, and a visible disconnected state.
+
+It forecloses the stateless server tier that polling would have allowed.
+
+The judgment is worth re-running if its inputs move: if operating the socket tier threatens the 99.5 percent availability goal, if connection counts grow well beyond the pilot or multi-region operation becomes a goal, or if update latency turns out not to matter to coordinators in practice. Polling remains the fallback closest to hand, because the change store's replay of revisions after a given revision is the same query a polling browser would make.
